{"id":925017,"date":"2026-09-10T16:12:39","date_gmt":"2026-09-10T20:12:39","guid":{"rendered":"https://www.nasa.gov/?p=925017"},"modified":"2026-09-10T16:14:01","modified_gmt":"2026-09-10T20:14:01","slug":"nasas-life-saving-technology-where-cell-signals-cant-go","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https://www.nasa.gov/technology/tech-transfer-spinoffs/nasas-life-saving-technology-where-cell-signals-cant-go/","title":{"rendered":"NASA&#8217;s Life-Saving Technology Where Cell Signals Can&#8217;t Go"},"content":{"rendered":"<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-article-hero-header nasa-gb-align-full bg-carbon-90 width-full maxw-full color-mode-dark hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-article-hero-header\">\t\t<div class=\"hds-cover-wrapper width-full maxw-full minh-tablet grid-container minh-tablet flex-column padding-0\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"hds-foreground-wrapper display-flex flex-direction-column\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-container grid-container-block margin-top-auto width-full maxw-desktop-lg padding-y-9 padding-x-3 desktop:padding-x-3 z-400\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"z-400 grid-col-12 tablet:grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-7 z-400\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"margin-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"label color-spacesuit-white margin-bottom-2\">3 Min Read</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<h1 class=\"heading-41 line-height-md color-spacesuit-white-important\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tNASA&#8217;s Life-Saving Technology Where Cell Signals Can&#8217;t Go\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</h1>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 tablet:grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-5\"></div>\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"skrim-overlay skrim-left mobile-skrim-top z-200\"></div>\n\t\t\t\t\t<figure class=\"hds-media-background  \"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1536\" height=\"864\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?w=1536\" class=\"attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536\" alt=\"A group of people on a boat, several of them are wearing shirts with text reading &quot;U.S. Coast Guard&quot;\" style=\"transform: scale(1.2); transform-origin: 46% 53%; object-position: 46% 53%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png 1782w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?resize=300,169 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?resize=768,432 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?resize=1024,576 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?resize=1536,864 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?resize=400,225 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?resize=600,337 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?resize=900,506 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/on-coast-guard-boat.png?resize=1200,675 1200w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px\" /></figure>\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div class=\"padding-y-3 padding-x-3\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-container grid-container-block padding-x-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<figcaption class=\"hds-caption maxw-mobile desktop:padding-x-3\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0 color-carbon-30\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div><figcaption>Rescued after more than four hours in the water, Easton Barrett (center, red shorts) and his friend were picked up by the U.S. Coast Guard thanks to a personal locator beacon (PLB). The devise sends a distress signal to satellites that are relayed back to Earth, launching a rescue operation. </figcaption></div>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"hds-credits color-spacesuit-white-important\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<span>Credits: </span>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<span>Easton Barrett</span>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</figcaption>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t</div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Memorial Day weekend 2024 started with a blue sky and a mild three- to four-foot chop in the water off the Gulf Coast of Mississippi&nbsp;—&nbsp;a perfect day for a fishing competition. A team of five was about 40 miles offshore checking their sonar, and 30 seconds later the boat was gone. They were in the water struggling to pull on life jackets and grab the coolers as they bobbed up. When a boat sinks, survivors can be virtually invisible amid the vast expanse of water.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When their fishing trip went wrong, Easton Barrett had the only mobile phone and no cell service. He recorded a brief farewell, planning to put his phone in a cooler in hopes someone would find it.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Another team member activated a personal locator beacon (PLB) that had been stowed at the last minute, which sent a distress signal to the Search and Rescue Satellite-Aided Tracking (SARSAT) technology carried by multiple satellites in Earth orbit. In the SARSAT system, developed partly by NASA, an emergency signal containing the transmitter’s location is directed to the nearest available ground station.</p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-none \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1574\" height=\"2048\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?w=1574\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"A bearded man stands holding three bright green devices in front of a bag, each has the logo for ACR on it.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png 3730w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=231,300 231w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=768,999 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=787,1024 787w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=1181,1536 1181w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=1574,2048 1574w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=308,400 308w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=461,600 461w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=692,900 692w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=923,1200 923w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/image-2.png?resize=1538,2000 1538w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1574px) 100vw, 1574px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">406 megahertz is the wavelength dedicated for PLB distress signals. On the annual 406 Day, Easton Barrett posts videos and messages on his social media accounts to help raise awareness about essential survival gear.</div><div class=\"hds-credits\">Credit: ACR</div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A mission control center then alerts rescue coordination centers to mobilize search and rescue crews. For Barrett and his crew, that was a Florida Coast Guard boat.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“Ever since, I have tried to teach others about safety on the water and in the outdoors by using a PLB,” said Barrett. “If that will save one life, it’s worth the effort.”</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A beacon like the one that saved his crew, a registered ResQLink PLB developed by ACR Electronics Inc. of Fort Lauderdale, Florida, also notifies the device owner’s emergency contact, indicating a distress call was activated. All emergency beacons must meet the same requirements to ensure they work when needed. Every rugged, buoyant, handheld devices have a five- to 10-year battery life.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">SARSAT began operations in 1982, becoming an international collaboration in 1985. The flight and ground technologies used globally were originally developed at NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland. Now there are 62 satellites in the program and 45 nations contributing services, from operating ground stations to providing rescue crews. More than 63,000 lives have been saved.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"2048\" height=\"1154\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?w=2048\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"A close up picture of a green ACR PLB atop a bag in a forest setting\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 55%; object-position: 50% 55%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg 5184w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=300,169 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=768,433 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=1024,577 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=1536,865 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=2048,1154 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=400,225 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=600,338 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=900,507 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=1200,676 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/acr-resqlink-view-nearshore-007-e1762797700405.jpeg?resize=2000,1127 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">Turning on a ResQLink View PLB from ACR Electronics will automatically “ping” orbiting satellites that send location and GPS information\nto the nearest search and rescue station. Whether on land or water, the appropriate resources will be dispatched to help anyone in distress\nanywhere in the world. </div><div class=\"hds-credits\">Credit: ACR</div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">SARSAT by the Numbers</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Search and Rescue Satellite-Aided Tracking system developed over several decades by NASA and other government agencies saves lives on land or at sea.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>1982&nbsp;—&nbsp;the start of U.S. operations</li>\n\n\n\n<li>1985&nbsp;—&nbsp;the start of international operations</li>\n\n\n\n<li>62 operational satellites</li>\n\n\n\n<li>45 nations contributing services</li>\n\n\n\n<li>63,000+ lives saved</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One rescue in 2024 demonstrates how it all comes together.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>40 miles off the Mississippi Gulf Coast</li>\n\n\n\n<li>5-person team participating in a fishing competition&nbsp;</li>\n\n\n\n<li>30 seconds for a boat to sink</li>\n\n\n\n<li>200 pounds of bait dumped to make a cooler buoyant</li>\n\n\n\n<li>3 close encounters with wildlife, likely sharks and eels</li>\n\n\n\n<li>4 hours in the water</li>\n\n\n\n<li>1 personal locator beacon</li>\n\n\n\n<li>1 Coast Guard rescue boat</li>\n\n\n\n<li>5 lives saved</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"nasa-gb-align-center padding-y-3 maxw-full width-full display-flex flex-align-center hds-module aligncenter wp-block-nasa-blocks-blockquote\"><div class=\"grid-container grid-container-block display-flex flex-column flex-justify-center padding-0\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:display-flex mobile:display-block\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"blockquote-icon margin-bottom-3\">\n\t\t\t\t<svg class=\"tablet:square-4 square-4 margin-right-3\" version=\"1.1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" x=\"0px\" y=\"0px\" viewBox=\"0 0 3000 3000\" style=\"enable-background:new 0 0 3000 3000;\" xml:space=\"preserve\"> <g> <path d=\"M586.7,1429.7c-10.7,1.5-21.4,2.8-33.9,4.5c6.9-26.6,12.7-50.7,19.5-74.6c32.4-114.1,78.5-222.2,146.8-319.5 c90.2-128.5,202.5-235.3,327.7-329.1c8.4-6.3,16.7-12.6,25.3-19.1c-66.3-105.1-131.5-208.6-197.3-313.1c-3.5,1.2-5.5,1.6-7.2,2.6 C714.4,469,576.1,575.7,456,705.3c-126,135.9-226.2,289.1-303,457.8c-98.8,217.1-151.3,444-147.2,683.3 c1.7,100.5,12.9,199.6,41.1,296.3C93.7,2303,182.2,2433,326.7,2520.1c176.9,106.7,366.8,126.8,563.4,70.5 c150.9-43.2,260.9-138.9,327.2-282.5c33.4-72.5,47.8-149.4,52-228.7